Q: How to make SwiftData @Query updating from changes made on a background context? A: <p>I'm going to expand on Geri's answer above here with a little explanation.</p>
<p>It seems that the only thing SwiftData looks at when determining if an object needs to be updated, is if its id (as represented by the <code>Identifiable</code> protocol) has changed. Now, an object's id can be anything you feel like. Making it a string comprised of all the elements in the object, as they suggest, works because any change to the data causes the id value to change.</p>
<p>But what if that's not possible? For example, what if you want to store image data in the object? (Not the best practice I know, but bear with me!) What if your object contains a LOT of information?</p>
<p>Here's an alternative. We use a GUID instead of a string. To make this easily implemented across all database objects you could use a base class, but I prefer to use a protocol, like this:</p>
<pre><code>protocol UUIDIdentifiable {
    var id: UUID { get set }
}

@Model
final class MyObject: UUIDIdentifiable {
    #Unique&lt;MyObject&gt;([\.id])
    var id: UUID
    init() {
        self.id = UUID()
        // initialise other variables here
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>I then create a singleton class with the sole purpose of watching for when model contexts are about to save. This object parses over any modified objects that conform to <code>UUIDIdentifiable</code> and sets a new UUID to this value to indicate that they have changed.</p>
<pre><code>class DatabaseObserver {

    public static let shared = DatabaseObserver()

    func startListening() {
        NotificationCenter.default.addObserver(
            self, selector: #selector(contextSaved),
            name: ModelContext.willSave, object: nil
        )
    }

    @objc func contextSaved(notification: NSNotification) {
        guard let context = notification.object as? ModelContext else {
            return
        }

        for var item in context.changedModelsArray.compactMap({ $0 as? UUIDIdentifiable }) {
            item.id = UUID()
        }
    }

}
</code></pre>
<p>Almost forgot: because static constants are lazily loaded, you need to ensure that the database observer is created when the app starts.</p>
<pre><code>@main
struct MyApp: App {   
    init() {
        DatabaseObserver.shared.startListening()
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Although this is rather longer, I greatly prefer it personally since it means that the object's identifier will be much shorter and you won't need to interpolate a long string with a lot of values in order to retrieve it.</p>
